That's a fair concern. If you can't rely on Node 18+, here are some alternatives:

**Simple workaround:**
```javascript
// Instead of array.findLast(predicate)
array.reverse().find(predicate)
```
Just note this mutates the array, so you'd want to do `[...array].reverse().find(predicate)` if you need to preserve the original.

**Or a traditional approach:**
```javascript
function findLast(array, predicate) {
  for (let i = array.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (predicate(array[i], i, array)) {
      return array[i];
    }
  }
  return undefined;
}
```

**Or if you want to be thorough:**
You could add a polyfill at the top of your project that only runs if `findLast` doesn't already exist, so you get native performance on newer Node versions but still support older ones.

Which approach fits best with your codebase?
